; matrix -- vectors and matrices as literals, computed exactly with `calc on`
;
;     [1, 2, 3]                  a row (a vector)
;     [[1], [3], [5]]            a column
;     [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]     a 2x3 matrix, row by row (also one row per line, see below)
;
; With `calc on`, `+`, `-`, `·` (`\cdot`: a number times a matrix, or a matrix times a matrix),
; `transpose` and `det` compute literals of numbers exactly (fractions), and `=`, `≠` compare
; them -- shapes that don't fit give no value. The entries are numbers; the calculator is trusted
; code that comes with Kurt (doc/kurt-doc.md, `calc`).
;
; A structure with its own operators: not together with numbers.kurt (a symbol is declared by one
; file only). This theory computes with literals and has no laws for matrices written with
; variables; for those, see proofs/mafi1/matrices.kurt (matrices over a field).
load equality
brackets [ ]
infix + 60 60
infix - 60 60
prefix - 72
infix · 70 70
infix / 70 70
arity transpose 1, det 1
flat +, ·
sym +

builtin [ matrix
builtin + add, - subtract, - negate, · multiply, / divide, transpose transpose, det determinant
builtin = eq, ≠ ne
